# Copy to apps/api/.env (gitignored):
#   cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env
#
# The credentials here must match infra/.env, which is what actually
# initialises the Postgres container. Full local setup from a fresh clone:
#
#   1. cp infra/.env.example infra/.env       # then edit POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#   2. cp apps/api/.env.example apps/api/.env # set the same password below
#   3. docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml up -d
#   4. cd apps/api && bun run db:migrate
#   5. bun test                               # also works from the repo root
#
# If you change POSTGRES_PASSWORD after the volume already exists, Postgres
# will NOT pick it up — credentials are only applied on first initialisation.
# Recreate the volume (destroys local data) with:
#   docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.yml down -v
DATABASE_URL=postgres://diudara:change_me@localhost:5432/diudara
PORT=3000

# REQUIRED, and not optional in the way it looks. Nothing else in this
# repository sets NODE_ENV — Bun does not default it, there is no `start`
# script, no Dockerfile, and no API service in infra/docker-compose.yml — so
# without this line `process.env.NODE_ENV` is `undefined`.
#
# bootstrap() treats NODE_ENV as an ALLOWLIST: the fake payment adapter and an
# absent XENDIT_CALLBACK_TOKEN are permitted ONLY when it is exactly
# "development" or "test" (`bun test` sets "test" itself). Every other value —
# including unset, "staging", "prod" and "PRODUCTION" — either refuses to
# start (messaging: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN/FONNTE_API_TOKEN) or, for Xendit
# specifically, DISABLES payments rather than refusing to start — see the
# Xendit block below. That is deliberate: an unrecognised value must fail
# CLOSED rather than silently taking fake money.
#
# On a real deployment, set the real Xendit variables below to accept paid
# communities; do NOT set NODE_ENV=development there to make a messaging
# error go away.
NODE_ENV=development

# Signing secret for creator session JWTs. Generate your own:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# The API refuses to start if this is unset, shorter than 32 characters, or
# still the placeholder value below — every creator session depends on this one
# key, and a weak HS256 secret is brute-forceable offline from a single token.
JWT_SECRET=change_me_to_a_long_random_string

# Xendit. Unset selects the fake payment adapter, which is permitted ONLY when
# NODE_ENV is exactly development or test (all tests rely on it). For every
# other NODE_ENV — including unset — leaving BOTH unset now DISABLES payments
# rather than refusing to start (Task 2, free communities): `bootstrap()`
# boots with no payment provider at all, POST /c/:slug/checkout is not even
# REGISTERED (a request to it 404s, not a fake invoice and not a 500), and
# POST /payment-account answers 503. Communities on a box like this must use
# `access_mode = "request"` — CreateCommunity/UpdateCommunity refuse
# `accessMode: "paid"` with a 409 while payments are disabled, so a priced
# membership can never be silently given away for free.
#
# Partial configuration (one set, the other unset) still THROWS in EVERY
# environment, unchanged: it is never intentional, and it makes an operator
# believe payments are live. Once BOTH are set, real money moves and a
# misconfigured box must not silently take fake payments and write
# fake-acct-* ids into creator.xendit_account_id, which cannot be undone
# without manual SQL — that is still what the fake adapter's own
# development/test-only restriction guards against.
# XENDIT_SECRET_KEY=
# XENDIT_SPLIT_RULE_ID=
# The static token Xendit sends as the X-CALLBACK-TOKEN header on every webhook.
# It is the ONLY thing authenticating POST /webhooks/xendit — Xendit signs nothing.
# Same thresholds as the two variables above: unset is fine when NODE_ENV is
# development or test (the webhook route then rejects every delivery, so set it if
# you want to exercise that path locally), AND — mirroring the Xendit keys
# themselves — unset is now also fine for any other NODE_ENV, PROVIDED
# XENDIT_SECRET_KEY/XENDIT_SPLIT_RULE_ID are also both unset (payments
# disabled: no invoice will ever exist for this webhook to authenticate).
# bootstrap() still THROWS for any other NODE_ENV if the two keys above ARE
# set without this one — real invoices with no way to authenticate the
# callback means nobody who pays is ever activated. It must also be at least
# 32 characters, exactly like JWT_SECRET above and for the same reason. Copy
# the value from Settings → Developers → Webhooks in the Xendit dashboard.
# The token the test suite uses is committed to this repository, so it is
# rejected here too.
# XENDIT_CALLBACK_TOKEN=

# Messaging providers — how a paying member actually receives access.
#
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN is issued by @BotFather (free and instant) and is what
# TelegramBotAdapter uses to create single-use, expiring invite links and to
# remove members. FONNTE_API_TOKEN is what FonnteWhatsAppAdapter uses to send the
# member the WhatsApp message carrying that link. WhatsApp is NOTIFICATION-ONLY:
# it cannot gate group access, and FonnteWhatsAppAdapter throws rather than
# pretending otherwise — see the WHY NOTIFY ONLY note in its source.
#
# Same rule as the Xendit variables above, and for the same reason: leaving one
# unset selects FakeMessagingAdapter, which records sends instead of making them,
# and that is permitted ONLY when NODE_ENV is exactly development or test. For
# every other value — INCLUDING unset, "staging", "prod" and "PRODUCTION" — the
# selector THROWS, because a box that looks like it is inviting paying members
# and is only writing to an array is the worst failure mode in this phase: the
# member appears granted and is not.
#
# BOTH processes select these, and both refuse to start without them outside the
# allowlist. The outbox worker (apps/worker) does the GRANTING — the API never
# issues an invite, it only writes the outbox row — while the API does the
# REVOKING, because a creator removing a member expects a synchronous answer.
# Both tokens are bearer credentials: the Telegram one is part of every Bot API
# request PATH, so neither adapter ever puts a URL, a request, or a response body
# into an error message.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# FONNTE_API_TOKEN=

# The secret you pass as `secret_token` when you install the bot's webhook, and
# which Telegram then sends back in the X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token header on
# every update. It is the ONLY authentication on POST /webhooks/telegram.
#
# What that endpoint is for: banChatMember addresses a Telegram integer USER ID,
# and this product grants access with an invite link precisely because it has none
# — checkout only knows a WhatsApp number. The id becomes knowable at the moment
# the member joins, which Telegram reports in a `chat_member` update carrying the
# invite link they used. Since every link is single-use per member, that link
# identifies exactly one channel_membership row, and the endpoint writes the user id
# onto it. WITHOUT THIS, channel_membership.external_member_id stays NULL forever
# and a creator can never remove a member who stops paying.
#
# So the rules mirror XENDIT_CALLBACK_TOKEN exactly: unset is fine when NODE_ENV is
# development or test (the endpoint then rejects every delivery), bootstrap() THROWS
# for any other NODE_ENV, and it throws in EVERY environment if TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
# is set without it. At least 32 characters, and only A-Z a-z 0-9 _ - (Telegram's
# setWebhook refuses anything else). Generate one:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# The secret the test suite uses is committed to this repository, so it is rejected
# here too.
#
# Installing it, once the API is reachable over HTTPS (a tunnel works locally —
# `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3000`):
#   curl "https://api.telegram.org/bot<TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN>/setWebhook" \
#     -d "url=https://<your-host>/webhooks/telegram" \
#     -d "secret_token=<TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET>" \
#     -d 'allowed_updates=["chat_member"]'
# `allowed_updates` must include chat_member: Telegram does NOT send it by default.
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# How often the outbox worker (apps/worker) looks for rows to send, in
# milliseconds. This is the delay a paying member sees between their payment
# settling and their invite arriving, so smaller is friendlier; every tick is also
# one indexed query, so smaller is not free. Unset means 5000. A value that is not
# a positive whole number THROWS rather than defaulting: Number("abc") is NaN, and
# setTimeout treats NaN as 0 — a worker hammering the database instead of polling
# it.
# WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=5000

# How often the SAME worker runs Phase 5's renewal and churn passes, in
# milliseconds. Unset means 3600000 (one hour), and it is deliberately three orders
# of magnitude longer than the outbox interval above: those passes decide whole
# Asia/Jakarta calendar days, and `renewal_reminder`'s unique (subscription_id, stage)
# means the first pass of a WIB day claims that day's reminder while every later pass
# that day is a no-op. It is not 24 hours either — the loop measures from the previous
# pass, not from a time of day, so a daily interval would pin every member's reminder
# to whatever time the worker last restarted. See DEFAULT_RENEWAL_INTERVAL_MS in
# apps/worker/src/scheduled-passes.ts. Same fail-closed parsing as above.
# WORKER_RENEWAL_INTERVAL_MS=3600000

# AI co-builder (Phase 7). Unlike Xendit/Telegram/Fonnte above, an absent key
# here does NOT block boot — the product works fine without a co-builder
# (design spec §11). Absence selects the fake adapter in development/test
# (same RELAXED_NODE_ENVS allowlist as everywhere else), and in every other
# NODE_ENV the API boots normally with the AI chat screen hidden rather than
# offering a button that always errors. This is deliberately the OPPOSITE
# failure mode from payments/messaging: those must fail closed because real
# money or a real invite is on the line; this one degrades gracefully because
# nothing is on the line if the co-builder is simply unavailable.
#
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY is a bearer credential (sent as "Authorization: Bearer
# <key>") — same handling rule as TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN/FONNTE_API_TOKEN above:
# OpenRouterAiAdapter never puts it, the request, or the raw response body
# into a thrown error message. OPENROUTER_MODEL is the OpenRouter model id to
# call (e.g. "openai/gpt-4o-mini" or "anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet" — see
# https://openrouter.ai/models for the current catalogue). Both must be set
# together or not at all; wiring this pair into bootstrap()'s selector is
# Task 5's job, not this file's.
#
# OpenRouterAiAdapter is UNVERIFIED against the live API — see the warning
# comment at the top of openrouter-ai.adapter.ts. There is no key anywhere in
# this repository's history to test one against.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=

# Forces FakeAiAdapter.nextBehaviour when no OpenRouter key is configured
# (RELAXED_NODE_ENVS only — has no effect at all once a real key is set, and
# no effect in production, where the fake is never constructed). Exists so a
# creator driving the co-builder chat screen in a REAL BROWSER can reach the
# fake's hostile-payload paths — a refusal, a prompt-injection attempt, a
# truncated/prose non-JSON reply (-> 502 after one retry), a provider
# timeout (-> 503) — none of which the fake's own hardcoded "draft" default
# ever produces. One of: draft, reply-only, prose, truncated-json,
# fenced-json, refusal, injection, timeout (see fake-ai.adapter.ts). An
# unrecognised value throws rather than silently falling back to "draft".
# AI_FAKE_BEHAVIOUR=draft

# Per-creator daily message cap on the AI co-builder (Task 5's spend guard —
# see AiUsageRepositoryPort). Unset means 50. A value that is not a positive
# whole number THROWS rather than defaulting, same fail-closed parsing as
# WORKER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS: Number("abc") is NaN, and a cap silently coerced to
# NaN would make every message_count < NaN comparison false — "allow
# nothing", not "no cap".
# AI_DAILY_MESSAGE_LIMIT=50

# Live streaming (MediaMTX). Unlike Xendit/Telegram/Fonnte above, an absent
# configuration here does NOT block boot — the product works fine without
# streaming, exactly like the AI co-builder above and for the same reason
# (design spec §7): absence selects FakeStreamingAdapter in development/test
# (same RELAXED_NODE_ENVS allowlist as everywhere else) and in every other
# NODE_ENV the API boots normally with the creator's streaming UI hidden
# rather than offering a "go live" button that always fails.
#
# All FIVE of the variables below are set together or not at all. Partial
# configuration (any one to four set, but not all five) THROWS in EVERY
# environment — a host with no webhook secret is a webhook nothing can
# authenticate, and a webhook secret with no host is a signing key naming a
# stream nobody can reach.
#
# MEDIAMTX_RTMP_HOST is what a creator's OBS is told to publish to:
#   rtmp://<MEDIAMTX_RTMP_HOST>:1935/live/<stream key>
# MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL is what a member's browser is told to play from — the
# public https:// origin nginx proxies to MediaMTX's HLS port (which itself
# stays bound to 127.0.0.1; see infra/mediamtx.yml, Task 6). No trailing
# slash needed; one is stripped if present.
# MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL (Task 2) is what a creator's BROWSER is told to
# publish to, as a WebRTC alternative to OBS's RTMP above:
#   <MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL>/whip/<stream key>
# This shape does NOT parallel MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL's — nginx serves `/whip/`
# as its own top-level location, deliberately NOT nested under `/live/`,
# because nginx's existing `^~ /live/` prefix match would permanently shadow
# anything placed beneath it. Verified against a real MediaMTX instance and a
# real publish (Task 1) before this adapter was written. No trailing slash
# needed; one is stripped if present.
# MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL and MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL must be the SAME public
# origin — nginx serves both `/live/` and `/whip/` off one server block — but
# nothing checks that for you; they are two independent strings a deploy can
# set inconsistently with no error until a creator tries browser publishing.
# MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET authenticates BOTH MediaMTX webhooks (Task 4) —
# the ONLY thing standing in for Xendit's X-CALLBACK-TOKEN on this pair of
# endpoints. STREAM_TOKEN_SECRET signs every watch token
# (apps/api/src/domain/watch-token.ts) and must NEVER be the same value as
# JWT_SECRET: different audience, different lifetime, and a compromise of
# one must not compromise the other.
# Both secrets must be at least 32 characters, exactly like JWT_SECRET above
# and for the same reason — each is the only thing standing between a
# stranger and a paid stream. Generate them separately:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# MEDIAMTX_RTMP_HOST=
# MEDIAMTX_HLS_BASE_URL=
# MEDIAMTX_WHIP_BASE_URL=
# MEDIAMTX_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# STREAM_TOKEN_SECRET=

# Email (Task 4, Resend). Unlike Xendit/Telegram/Fonnte above, an absent
# configuration here does NOT block boot — the SAME divergence
# selectPaymentProvider makes for the reason free communities gave it: Task
# 5's password reset falls back to WhatsApp when a user has a number and
# messaging is configured, and sends nothing when neither channel is
# available, so refusing to start over a missing email provider would let an
# OPTIONAL channel take down account recovery entirely.
#
# Absence selects FakeEmailAdapter in development/test (same RELAXED_NODE_ENVS
# allowlist as everywhere else — the fake records sends into an array instead
# of making them). In every other NODE_ENV — including unset, "staging" and
# "PRODUCTION" — absence DISABLES email rather than refusing to start:
# selectEmailProvider returns null, never the fake adapter, so Task 5 can see
# there is no email channel and try WhatsApp instead of silently believing a
# send happened.
#
# Partial configuration (one set, the other unset) still THROWS in EVERY
# environment, unchanged from every other provider pair in this file: it is
# never intentional, and it makes an operator believe email is live.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY is a bearer credential (sent as "Authorization: Bearer
# <key>") — same handling rule as TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN/OPENROUTER_API_KEY
# above: ResendEmailAdapter never puts it, the request, or the raw response
# body into a thrown error message. EMAIL_FROM is the "From" address/name
# Resend sends as, e.g. "DIUDARA <no-reply@yourdomain.example>" — it must be
# a domain verified in your Resend dashboard.
#
# ResendEmailAdapter is UNVERIFIED against the live API — see the warning
# comment at the top of resend-email.adapter.ts. There is no key anywhere in
# this repository's history to test one against.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=

# Image storage (Phase 4, Task 2) — Biznet Gio NEO Object Storage, or any
# other S3-compatible bucket. Get the five values below from the Biznet Gio
# NEO portal's Access page (Object Storage -> your bucket -> Access), which
# issues the access key ID/secret pair and shows the bucket's own endpoint
# and region.
#
# UNLIKE every other absence-tolerant provider in this file (Xendit above is
# the one exception, and streaming/AI/email are the graceful-degradation
# ones), a missing bucket here BLOCKS BOOT outside NODE_ENV=development/test,
# the same allowlist as everywhere else. That is a deliberate asymmetry: an
# API that accepts image uploads and quietly keeps them in a Map that vanishes
# on the next restart or deploy is worse than one that refuses to start —
# nothing tells a creator their post's photo is gone until a follower reports
# a broken image. Absence selects FakeMediaStorageAdapter ONLY in
# development/test (the fake keeps bytes in memory instead of a bucket, which
# is fine for a process that is expected to restart constantly and never
# needs the bytes to survive that).
#
# All FIVE variables are set together or not at all — partial configuration
# THROWS in EVERY environment, same reasoning as every other provider pair in
# this file: an access key with no bucket is never intentional.
#
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID and S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are bearer credentials — same
# handling rule as RESEND_API_KEY/OPENROUTER_API_KEY above:
# S3MediaStorageAdapter never puts them, or a request, or a raw response body,
# into a thrown error message. S3_BUCKET is the bucket name. S3_ENDPOINT is
# the S3-compatible endpoint (Biznet Gio NEO's is region-specific, e.g.
# https://s3.id-jkt-1.neo.id) — this adapter always passes an explicit
# endpoint, never AWS's default, because Biznet Gio NEO is not AWS.
# S3_REGION is the region id (e.g. id-jkt-1).
#
# The key layout (posts/<id>/full.webp, posts/<id>/thumb.webp) lives ONLY
# inside s3-media-storage.adapter.ts — nothing else in this codebase composes
# a bucket key or a URL, which is what keeps a bucket URL from ever escaping
# into an HTTP response (design spec §5.1, Phase 6's paywall depends on it).
#
# S3MediaStorageAdapter is UNVERIFIED against a live bucket — see the warning
# comment at the top of s3-media-storage.adapter.ts. There is no bucket
# anywhere in this repository's history to test one against.
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_REGION=

# Base URL of the PUBLIC web app (apps/web), used to build the
# success_redirect_url Xendit sends the payer to after paying:
#   <APP_BASE_URL>/c/<slug>/status/<subscriptionId>
# Without it the confirmation page is unreachable — a member who pays is left on
# Xendit's own receipt with no way back. Defaults to the Vite dev server below;
# set it to the real https:// origin on a deployment. No trailing slash.
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173
